id: question-packets/mascot
title: "Mascot ranked questions"
kind: question-packet
status: proposed
updated: 2026-09-07

purpose: >
  Hold the stack-ranked questions the YAWN.bot mascot system must answer, in
  the same shape yawn.bot/frontier renders: one governing question, ranked
  entries, each with the current answer, the case against it, the proof that
  would settle it, and the loop status. Public entries render on the
  unprovisioned /mascot view; every entry renders for a provisioned owner.

target_contract: interface/yawn-mascot-v0.1.yawn
render_format: "ranked details/summary list (yawn.bot/frontier pattern); only the top rank open by default"
ranking_rule: "ranked by how much the answer changes Dave's next move; the ranking is a bet, not evidence"

governing_question: "What is the YAWN.bot mascot, and how does it know what it is doing?"

honesty_line: >
  These answers are proposals with their status attached. An answer marked
  inferred or reported has not been proved in this runtime yet.

rules:
  - "A question with no answer stays visible with answer: null."
  - "An answer never upgrades its own epistemic status; proof does."
  - "public_safe: false entries never render on the unprovisioned view."
  - "loop_status uses the six-stage loop: signal | orientation | choice | move | proof | update."

questions:
  - rank: 1
    key: universal_browser_technology
    question: "What is the best universal browser technology for a state-driven pixel mascot in the YAWN.bot Next.js app?"
    answer: null  # TODO-RESEARCH
    epistemic_status: unknown
    case_against: null
    proof: "A header mark, a state card, and a 200-body world view run on iOS Safari, Android Chrome, and desktop with one shared ticker, crisp pixels, reduced-motion stills, and zero route-owned console errors."
    loop_status: orientation
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "Sprite manifest the runtime already detects", href: "src/domain/sprite-sheets.ts" }

  - rank: 2
    key: claude_technology
    question: "What technology does Claude use for the pixel mascot and the animated Ultracode band?"
    answer: null  # TODO-RESEARCH
    epistemic_status: unknown
    case_against: "Even a confirmed answer would describe Anthropic's stack, not ours; the useful part is the two-layer split (field plus sprite), which is already observable."
    proof: "A primary source (Anthropic engineering post, talk, or the public package bundle) names the mechanism."
    loop_status: orientation
    public_safe: true
    links: []

  - rank: 3
    key: how_state_is_known
    question: "How does the mascot know what state it is in?"
    answer: >
      It does not decide; it reads. One pure function resolves the loop
      status, worker expression state, mirror-face expression, world
      execution state, header status, and inbox status that the runtime
      already keeps into {state, variant, flags} plus a replayable input
      hash. Hard-gate flags outrank needs-owner, which outranks the loop
      status, which outranks micro-expressions. Missing inputs resolve to
      sleeping with an unknown badge; nothing is guessed.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: "Six source enums drift independently; a mapping table can silently rot. The mitigation is the exhaustive state-map test that fails on any unmapped value."
    proof: "The state-map test enumerates every value of every input enum; adding a value without a mapping fails CI."
    loop_status: choice
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "Loop statuses", href: "src/domain/yawn-state-substrate.ts" }
      - { label: "Worker expression state", href: "src/domain/automation-registry.ts" }
      - { label: "Mirror face expression", href: "src/domain/yawn-frame-grid.ts" }

  - rank: 4
    key: which_states
    question: "Which states exist, and what does each one look like?"
    answer: >
      Ten states: sleeping, orienting, asking, moving, working (a variant of
      moving for code, write, and read moves), proving, updating, holding,
      blocked, closed. Four flags overlay them: needs_owner, error,
      local_only, unknown_inputs. Two states already have sheets (sleeping
      from the idle sheet, moving from the walk sheets); the rest fall back
      to the nearest accepted ancestor until a sheet is promoted.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: "Ten states may be too many to read at 32 pixels; a swarm may only need four legible silhouettes. Keep the ten in the model, but prove legibility per size before generating ten sheets."
    proof: "A legibility test: five people name the state of each still frame at 32, 128, and 256 pixels; states below a threshold merge visually (not semantically)."
    loop_status: choice
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "State model", href: "interface/yawn-mascot-v0.1.yawn" }

  - rank: 5
    key: how_frames_are_made
    question: "How are the animation frames made?"
    answer: null  # TODO-RESEARCH: provider per stage, cost, post-processing
    epistemic_status: unknown
    case_against: null
    proof: "One state sheet travels reference pack -> brief hash -> approval receipt -> candidate -> post-process -> manifest -> promotion receipt, with every hash reconciling."
    loop_status: orientation
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "Image generator receipt idiom", href: "src/lib/yawn-image/generate.ts" }
      - { label: "Art engine", href: "nestheads-art-engine/README.md" }

  - rank: 6
    key: who_approves
    question: "Who approves generation, spend, and promotion?"
    answer: >
      The owner of the coordinate, per candidate, per state, with a receipt
      that names the brief hash, adapter, candidate count, spend ceiling,
      and expiry. Generation and promotion are separate receipts. Visual
      quality never approves anything; a nonconforming candidate ends its
      grant.
    epistemic_status: observed
    case_against: "Per-candidate approval is slow for ten states times several attempts. The answer is a bounded batch receipt (N candidates, one ceiling, one expiry), never standing authority."
    proof: "A generation attempt without a current receipt is refused and leaves an inspectable refusal record."
    loop_status: proof
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "Owner-decision receipt precedent", href: "records/interception-statement-image-approval-2026-08-29.yawn" }

  - rank: 7
    key: header_icon
    question: "What does the upper-left icon show, and to whom?"
    answer: >
      The current bot's mascot in its current state, with the state name as
      accessible text. On the public root it shows only the root bot's
      public state class. A signed-in owner on their coordinate sees that
      coordinate's bot, with flags.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: "A moving icon in every header is attention cost; the chrome contract says default surfaces foreground one state and one next action. The mark must stay quiet: small, slow idle, no motion under reduced-motion, and no motion at all for sleeping unless the user hovers or focuses."
    proof: "Signed-out Playwright run finds no private detail in the header DOM; reduced-motion run finds no running ticker."
    loop_status: choice
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "Chrome contract", href: "interface/yawn-chrome-v1.yawn" }

  - rank: 8
    key: holarchy_inheritance
    question: "How do child yawns and bots inherit the mascot?"
    answer: >
      By default every descendant uses the same state map and manifest
      schema, so a swarm reads consistently. Accepted projection
      preferences at principal, arena, or yawn scope may change which
      sheet or field pattern a state uses; exact-coordinate revisions win
      over ancestors. Private state, reasons, and receipts never inherit
      through nesting.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: "Inheritance makes a hundred identical bodies; identity may need to be per-yawn (color, hat, accessory) to stay legible. Roles and flags supply that variety without forking the state grammar."
    proof: "Holarchy inheritance tests: a child renders the parent's accepted default, an exact-coordinate override wins, and a private parent flag never reaches a public child."
    loop_status: choice
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "Holarchy inheritance policy", href: "YAWNS/Policies/YAWN_HOLARCHY_INHERITANCE.yawn" }

  - rank: 9
    key: hats_and_accessories
    question: "What do hats and accessories mean?"
    answer: >
      Hats encode role (root steward, objective steward, worker). Badges
      encode flags (needs owner, error, local, unknown). Tools encode the
      active move kind (terminal, pen, book, mail). Bubbles carry one short
      text label. An accessory adds a visual role and never remaps a state.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: "Accessory vocabularies grow without limit and stop meaning anything. Cap the vocabulary per kind and require a text meaning for every accessory in the manifest."
    proof: "Every accessory in a published manifest has a meaning string, and the state label still renders with the accessory hidden."
    loop_status: orientation
    public_safe: true
    links: []

  - rank: 10
    key: public_vs_provisioned
    question: "What does a public visitor see, and what does Dave see?"
    answer: >
      Public: the mascot in its public state, the state list with one still
      each, and the public-safe questions, in the /frontier format with no
      JavaScript required. Provisioned: everything public plus the full
      ranked set with cases against and proofs, live state resolution
      inputs, the manifest inspector, pending generation proposals with
      dry-run cost, and accepted defaults with their scope.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: "Two views of one route can leak by accident when a component forgets the boundary. Render the provisioned extras from a separate server component behind the session check, never by hiding client-side."
    proof: "Signed-out and signed-in Playwright receipts diff the DOM; the signed-out DOM contains none of the provisioned markers."
    loop_status: choice
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "Frontier page pattern", href: "src/app/frontier/page.tsx" }

  - rank: 11
    key: accessibility_and_cost
    question: "How does this stay accessible and cheap on phones?"
    answer: >
      One shared ticker per page; sprites share decoded textures; off-screen
      and hidden-tab bodies stop; every raster layer uses nearest-neighbor
      scaling; prefers-reduced-motion shows a still frame and a still field;
      the state name is always text; the header mark never holds a WebGL
      context.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: "CSS-only sprites cannot share a ticker, and canvas cannot be styled by CSS. The layer split (CSS for the small mark, canvas for the field and swarm) is the compromise; measure before deciding."
    proof: "A Playwright performance receipt on a throttled mobile profile: main-thread time per frame under budget with 50 bodies visible."
    loop_status: orientation
    public_safe: true
    links: []

  - rank: 12
    key: training
    question: "How do we train a model on our character?"
    answer: null  # TODO-RESEARCH: LoRA lane vs reference conditioning
    epistemic_status: unknown
    case_against: null
    proof: "A held-out state brief produces identity-stable frames against the reference pack under a fixed rubric, with cost and time recorded."
    loop_status: orientation
    public_safe: false
    links:
      - { label: "LoRA registry", href: "nestheads-art-engine/model/lora-registry.json" }

  - rank: 13
    key: truthfulness
    question: "What proves the mascot is telling the truth about state?"
    answer: >
      The resolved state, its inputs, and the input hash render together in
      the provisioned view, and the same inputs must resolve identically on
      every device. The mascot is a View: it cannot be more true than the
      runtime records it reads, and it cannot be less true without failing
      the replay check.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: "A stale input (a worker whose status did not update) makes an honest mascot show a wrong state. Freshness must be an input: stale inputs add the unknown badge."
    proof: "Replay test: recorded inputs replayed later resolve to the same state and hash; stale inputs older than the freshness window show the unknown badge."
    loop_status: proof
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "Projection boundary", href: "core/projection-and-aperture.yawn" }

  - rank: 14
    key: mascot_changes_pixels
    question: "Can the mascot change the pixels of the interface, or turn them into code?"
    answer: >
      As expression, yes: in the working state the field tiles drain into
      the terminal as code, and in updating the body dissolves and
      re-resolves. As authority, no: the mascot animates a View and never
      mutates semantic state, permissions, or records. The pixels it changes
      are its own layers.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: "The metaphor invites a mascot that appears to act, which is the hidden-authority problem in costume. The rule holds only if every visible change maps to a recorded event the user can inspect."
    proof: "Every animation transition corresponds to a state change with a source event; a transition with no source event is a test failure."
    loop_status: orientation
    public_safe: true
    links: []

  - rank: 15
    key: not_allowed
    question: "What is not allowed?"
    answer: >
      No writing state from the mascot. No color-only or motion-only state.
      No generation, spend, storage, or publication without a current
      receipt. No third-party mascot copies. No private detail on public
      surfaces. No preference that remaps a canonical state.
    epistemic_status: observed
    case_against: null
    proof: "Each rule has a test or a refusal record in the contract's proof section."
    loop_status: proof
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "Contract boundaries", href: "interface/yawn-mascot-v0.1.yawn" }

  - rank: 16
    key: first_move
    question: "What is the smallest first move?"
    answer: >
      Ship the state map and the header mark with the two existing sheets
      (sleeping from the idle sheet, moving from the walk sheet) and the
      CSS field band behind a route flag. No generation, no spend. Then
      generate one sheet (working) through the full receipt chain to prove
      the pipeline before the other seven.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: "Shipping the mark before the states exist trains users to ignore it. Keep the mark quiet until at least three states are legible."
    proof: "State-map test green; header mark visible with text label; reduced-motion still; public root unchanged; one promoted sheet with a reconciled receipt chain."
    loop_status: move
    public_safe: true
    links: []

replay:
  next_questions:
    - "Which two states, after sleeping and moving, most change what a viewer does next?"
    - "Does a per-yawn body or a per-parent accessory read better in dense views?"
